  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.transform_with_spanner - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.transform_with_spanner.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.transform_with_spanner()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Transform graph to a spanner with given stretch.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.transform_with_spanner()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Transform graph to a spanner with given stretch.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.transform_with_spanner()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Transform graph to a spanner with given stretch.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nTransform graph to a spanner with given stretch.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L53499-L53647){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.transform_with_spanner(\n    stretch,\n    weight_attr=None,\n    weighted=None,\n    seed=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Spanner](../assets/graphs/transform-with-spanner.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A six-node ladder with two diagonal shortcuts, A to E and B to F, in the accent color. The stretch-2 spanner drops both diagonals, shown dashed: each one is replaced by a two-step path, so no distance more than doubles.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA spanner is a sparse subgraph that approximates distances in the original graph. For any\npair of nodes `u` and `v`, the distance in the spanner is at most `stretch=` times the\ndistance in the original graph:\n\n`distance_spanner(u, v) ≤ stretch × distance_original(u, v)`\n\nThe resulting spanner preserves approximate distances while using fewer edges, making it\nuseful for graph compression, network backbone extraction, and approximate distance queries.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[stretch]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Maximum distance approximation factor. Must be ≥ `1.0`. Lower `stretch=` values give\n    better approximations but more edges. Higher `stretch=` values create sparser spanners.\n\n<code>[weight_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Edge attribute to use as distance in place of the weight field, which every edge must\n    hold as a number; `\"weight\"` names the weight field. If `None` (default), `weighted=`\n    decides.\n\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True`, edge weights (`1` where missing) are the distances. If `False`, every edge\n    has distance `1`. If `None` (default), follows whether the graph was created as\n    weighted. Setting `weight_attr=` implies `True`.\n\n<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Random seed for reproducibility. The algorithm has randomized components, so different\n    seeds may produce different spanners with the same stretch guarantee.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new graph with the same nodes but a subset of edges that forms a spanner with the\n    specified stretch.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If stretch < `1.0`, if graph is directed, or if `weight_attr=` names an attribute that\n    an edge lacks or holds as a non-number, or is combined with `weighted=False`.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nA $t$-spanner is a subgraph $H \\subseteq G$ that preserves every pairwise distance up to a\nmultiplicative factor: $d_H(u, v) \\le t \\cdot d_G(u, v)$ for all node pairs. This\nimplementation uses a greedy scan that considers edges in weight order and admits each one\nonly when the current spanner cannot already reach its endpoints within the stretch bound,\ncosting $O(mn)$ time. The Baswana-Sen (2007) randomized algorithm attains the same guarantee\nin expected linear time with a tighter sparsity bound but is trickier to implement. The\ngreedy variant here is chosen for clarity and determinism up to the tie-breaking controlled\nby `seed=`. For unweighted graphs the resulting spanner holds $O(n^{1 + 1/k})$ edges with\n$k = \\lfloor(t + 1)/2\\rfloor$, which is optimal assuming the Erdős girth conjecture.\nWeighted graphs may keep more edges but still dramatically fewer than\nthe original.\n\nSpanners are the workhorse behind graph compression, network-backbone extraction, and fast\napproximate distance queries, and they make an excellent preprocessing step before running\nan expensive shortest-path or diameter computation.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~shortest_path_length`) \n\n:   Compute the length of a shortest path.\n\n[](`~minimum_spanning_tree`) \n\n:   Find a spanning tree of least total weight.\n\n[](`~edge_subgraph`) \n\n:   Extract specific edges and their endpoints.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA spanner keeps only some of the edges, while no shortest path grows by more than a factor\nof `stretch=`. On a weighted graph, a long direct edge can go if a shorter route remains:\n\n::: {#78da063e .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ng = ec.Graph(weighted=True).add_nodes([\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"])\ng.add_edges([(\"A\", \"B\", 1.0), (\"B\", \"C\", 1.0), (\"A\", \"C\", 5.0)])\n\ng.transform_with_spanner(stretch=3.0).get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['A--B', 'B--C']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Fewer roads\n\n`ec.towns()` is a road network of 12 towns, with each road's length in km as its weight.\nAllowing every trip to be up to 50% longer, a spanner keeps 20 of the 26 roads. A larger\nstretch keeps fewer:\n\n::: {#18567515 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntowns = ec.towns()\n\n[\n    towns.transform_with_spanner(stretch=s).count_edges()\n    for s in (1.5, 2, 3)\n]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n[20, 15, 13]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nAll the roads on the left, the 20 the spanner keeps on the right. The roads it drops all\nhave a detour within the bound. The worst is Bramford to Halston, 47 km by road and 64 km\nwithout it:\n\n::: {#dde2136a .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nkept = towns.transform_with_spanner(stretch=1.5)\n\nkept.shortest_path_length(\"Bramford\", \"Halston\", weighted=True)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n64.0\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#2ce44495 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntowns.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\", width=360, height=360, legend=False\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"360\" height=\"360\" viewBox=\"12.5 17.2 345.5 345.5\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"12.5\